Here’s a quick Java‑style example that shows how you’d set up your `userProfile` map and then invoke the `XContentBuilder_map` helper to serialize it while guaranteeing no self‑references and wrapping the result in `{}`:

```java
// 1️⃣ Build the map you want to serialize
Map<String, Object> userProfile = new HashMap<>();
userProfile.put("name",  "John Doe");
userProfile.put("age",   30);
userProfile.put("email", "john@example.com");

// 2️⃣ Call the helper – note the arguments are passed as *strings* (the tool expects string representations)
functions.XContentBuilder_map({
    values: "userProfile",          // <-- the map variable name
    ensureNoSelfReferences: "true", // <-- prevent stack‑overflow from circular refs
    writeStartAndEndHeaders: "true" // <-- wrap the output in { … }
});
```

**What this does**

| Argument | Meaning |
|----------|---------|
| `values` | The actual map (`userProfile`) you’re serializing. |
| `ensureNoSelfReferences` | If the map contains a reference back to itself, the serializer will throw an exception instead of recursing forever. |
| `writeStartAndEndHeaders` | Adds the opening `{` and closing `}` around the serialized JSON, so you get a complete JSON object. |

That’s all you need—just replace the sample values with whatever data you have, and the helper will take care of the rest!

